WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.201 --> 00:00:02.502
-Our next guest
is a Tony-nominated

00:00:02.502 --> 00:00:03.937
and Emmy Award-winning actress

00:00:03.937 --> 00:00:05.337
you know from her work
in Broadway's

00:00:05.337 --> 00:00:06.639
"Wicked" and "Dreamgirls,"

00:00:06.639 --> 00:00:09.275
as well as shows like
"Ray Donovan" and "Moesha."

00:00:09.275 --> 00:00:10.944
She stars
in "Abbott Elementary,"

00:00:10.944 --> 00:00:12.812
which airs Wednesday nights
on ABC.

00:00:12.812 --> 00:00:14.080
Let's take a look.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:17.183
-Uh, what I'm seeing here is,
you guys just

00:00:17.183 --> 00:00:19.152
failed to pay attention
to what was happening.

00:00:19.152 --> 00:00:20.719
You have absolutely no case.

00:00:20.719 --> 00:00:23.590
And, in fact, according to this,
you might each owe

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:27.760
Legendary Chargers, yeah,
$200 for crew fees.

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:29.161
-For what?
-I don't --

00:00:29.161 --> 00:00:31.097
-Let them try to get the money.
-So you are saying

00:00:31.097 --> 00:00:33.332
that we're just gonna have to
see this commercial forever,

00:00:33.332 --> 00:00:35.501
and there's no way
we can get it off the air?

00:00:35.501 --> 00:00:38.037
-You could pay for Hulu
without commercials.

00:00:38.037 --> 00:00:39.739
Mm-hmm.

00:00:39.739 --> 00:00:41.674
-Please welcome to the show
the one, the only

00:00:41.674 --> 00:00:43.342
Sheryl Lee Ralph, everybody!

00:00:43.342 --> 00:00:45.011
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:45.011 --> 00:00:55.021 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:55.021 --> 00:01:05.064 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:05.965 --> 00:01:10.436
Welcome, Sheryl!
I am so happy to see you.

00:01:10.436 --> 00:01:12.871
I knew -- the last time
I saw you was at the Emmys.

00:01:12.871 --> 00:01:15.408
We talked before the show
about how you were coming on.

00:01:15.408 --> 00:01:17.743
We did not know that our paths
were going to cross again...

00:01:17.743 --> 00:01:18.978
-That's right.
-...over the course

00:01:18.978 --> 00:01:21.181
of the evening,
which is Amy Poehler and I

00:01:21.181 --> 00:01:23.649
presented the Emmy
for your category,

00:01:23.649 --> 00:01:26.819
and we were lucky enough to be
onstage when you came up.

00:01:26.819 --> 00:01:28.621
-Look at you.
-I was very happy for you.

00:01:28.621 --> 00:01:30.856
-Yes, very handsome.
-I was very happy.

00:01:30.856 --> 00:01:34.160
I was also very happy that you
showed up because, Sheryl,

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:35.828
you were so surprised you won,

00:01:35.828 --> 00:01:37.897
you sat in your chair
for a long time.

00:01:37.897 --> 00:01:39.732
-I did.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:39.732 --> 00:01:42.635
Seth, when they called my name,

00:01:42.635 --> 00:01:46.239
it was like everything
just turned to mush,

00:01:46.239 --> 00:01:48.675
and I was in some surreal place.

00:01:48.675 --> 00:01:50.810
And I was like,
"Is this really happening?

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:53.012
Did they really
just call my name?"

00:01:53.012 --> 00:01:58.384
And then I could hear
Quinta and Tyler say, "Get up!"

00:01:58.384 --> 00:01:59.986
[ Laughter ]

00:01:59.986 --> 00:02:01.487
-Well,
I love this photo 'cause, one,

00:02:01.487 --> 00:02:03.155
you can see
how happy everyone is,

00:02:03.155 --> 00:02:05.191
and, two, you can see that
you really...

00:02:05.191 --> 00:02:07.626
[ Laughter ]
Now, be honest.

00:02:07.626 --> 00:02:10.362
Did you have any -- so did you
just go into it thinking,

00:02:10.362 --> 00:02:12.064
"Oh, this is an honor
to be nominated,

00:02:12.064 --> 00:02:14.700
but I won't be up there
accepting an award tonight?"

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:16.402
-That is absolutely it.

00:02:16.402 --> 00:02:19.505
I mean, you spend your career,
you do the good work.

00:02:19.505 --> 00:02:22.876
You don't think about the fact
that, "Oh, I'm gonna get this,

00:02:22.876 --> 00:02:24.510
or I'm gonna get that,"
or whatever.

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:27.380
It would be nice,
but you really do the good work.

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:30.582
So when Oprah got up
on that stage and said,

00:02:30.582 --> 00:02:35.721
"Only 1 in 3,000 people
will ever have one of these,

00:02:35.721 --> 00:02:37.723
and you all are probably
not one of them"...

00:02:37.723 --> 00:02:41.027
[ Laughter ]
...I was like, "Okay.

00:02:41.027 --> 00:02:43.095
She set the bar."

00:02:43.095 --> 00:02:45.531
But then they called my name,

00:02:45.531 --> 00:02:49.836
and it was like my whole career
went in front of me.

00:02:49.836 --> 00:02:52.071
And I was like, "Oh, my God.

00:02:52.071 --> 00:02:54.874
I'm 1 in 3,000!"

00:02:54.874 --> 00:02:59.312
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:59.312 --> 00:03:04.550
-And then, I have heard since
that you got some, you know --

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:06.986
I believe Oprah herself
reached out after the fact.

00:03:06.986 --> 00:03:08.587
The Vice President reached out.

00:03:08.587 --> 00:03:10.189
-It's all true.

00:03:10.189 --> 00:03:12.492
-You played Deena Jones
on Broadway in "Dreamgirls."

00:03:12.492 --> 00:03:14.660
-Yes.
-And someone else played

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:18.331
the same role in cinema,
reached out to you.

00:03:18.331 --> 00:03:21.534
-Yes, baby.
Beyoncé sent me flowers!

00:03:21.534 --> 00:03:24.270
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:25.705
-Let me tell you.
That right there,

00:03:25.705 --> 00:03:28.574
that's a very nice note
to get from Beyoncé.

00:03:28.574 --> 00:03:29.942
That one goes
right in the scrapbook.

00:03:29.942 --> 00:03:32.578
-Yes, it does.
Yes, it does.

00:03:32.578 --> 00:03:36.115
And then I got the nice phone
call from the Vice President.

00:03:36.115 --> 00:03:39.318
And I was like,
"Kamala, we did it!

00:03:39.318 --> 00:03:42.755
Kamala, we did it!"
[ Laughter ]

00:03:42.755 --> 00:03:45.391
-Exactly.
It's full circle.

00:03:45.391 --> 00:03:46.625
-Absolutely.

00:03:46.625 --> 00:03:48.628
-Obviously,
you've been on stage.

00:03:48.628 --> 00:03:50.195
You've been in film
and television.

00:03:50.195 --> 00:03:51.697
"Abbott Elementary"
is a wonderful show.

00:03:51.697 --> 00:03:53.666
I guess it's a slightly
different style for you

00:03:53.666 --> 00:03:55.534
because it is this
mockumentary style.

00:03:55.534 --> 00:03:58.203
Did you find it --
Did you step right into it?

00:03:58.203 --> 00:03:59.572
Did you have to learn it?

00:03:59.572 --> 00:04:01.741
Because it seems so natural
watching you do it.

00:04:01.741 --> 00:04:03.376
-It's a bit of a learning curve.

00:04:03.376 --> 00:04:05.077
I mean,
the fact that the cameras --

00:04:05.077 --> 00:04:07.547
of course you know
the cameras are there.

00:04:07.547 --> 00:04:09.215
But the cameras are,
like, there.

00:04:09.215 --> 00:04:12.318
And the choreography,
they're, like, there.

00:04:12.318 --> 00:04:15.755
There were parts of me I did not
know people were seeing,

00:04:15.755 --> 00:04:17.690
and I was like,
"I'm getting to the gym."

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:19.492
[ Laughter ]

00:04:19.492 --> 00:04:22.060
-That's how they get you.
-That's how they get you.

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:24.830
I was like, "Oh, my God!"

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:27.633
But it was all right, you know.
We had a good time.

00:04:27.633 --> 00:04:29.969
And I love our camera crew.

00:04:29.969 --> 00:04:32.405
They literally
have their own choreography.

00:04:32.405 --> 00:04:34.807
They have these little
squeegee stools

00:04:34.807 --> 00:04:38.176
that they work on, and they can
spin around and do crazy things,

00:04:38.176 --> 00:04:39.712
and they're just so good.

00:04:39.712 --> 00:04:41.613
They're like a part of the cast,
in a weird way.

00:04:41.613 --> 00:04:44.483
-The response to this show
has been phenomenal

00:04:44.483 --> 00:04:45.851
across all walks of life.

00:04:45.851 --> 00:04:48.153
But one of the things
I keep hearing that must be

00:04:48.153 --> 00:04:50.089
so rewarding for everybody
involved in the show

00:04:50.089 --> 00:04:52.125
is how much teachers love it.

00:04:52.125 --> 00:04:53.559
Do you just hear constantly

00:04:53.559 --> 00:04:55.060
from people
in the teaching profession?

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:58.865
-I constantly hear from
all kinds of educators --

00:04:58.865 --> 00:05:01.433
teachers, principals, everybody.

00:05:01.433 --> 00:05:03.569
But especially
the teachers saying,

00:05:03.569 --> 00:05:06.539
"Finally, we are not
the butt of the joke.

00:05:06.539 --> 00:05:09.341
We are the heart of the show."

00:05:09.341 --> 00:05:10.409
-Yeah.
-And it's --

00:05:10.409 --> 00:05:11.543
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:11.543 --> 00:05:14.680
-Thank you.
And it's just --

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:17.883
it's just so wonderful
to hear from them,

00:05:17.883 --> 00:05:21.353
and when they say things
to you like, "I feel seen.

00:05:21.353 --> 00:05:24.390
I feel understood,"
and I'm like,

00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:27.660
"Honeys, everybody got a feeling
for what you all go through

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:29.328
through the pandemic."
-Yeah.

00:05:29.328 --> 00:05:31.998
-All those people
sending those messages,

00:05:31.998 --> 00:05:35.367
"I had no idea my child
was so bad!"

00:05:35.367 --> 00:05:37.537
[ Laughter ]
"Oh, my God!

00:05:37.537 --> 00:05:39.472
I didn't know
this is what you go through!

00:05:39.472 --> 00:05:43.075
I'm sorry I didn't show up
to the parent-teacher meeting!"

00:05:43.075 --> 00:05:44.376
All of that.

00:05:44.376 --> 00:05:47.780
But I know, finally,
they know that we understand.

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:51.884
And honest to God, teachers
really deserve much more.

00:05:51.884 --> 00:05:54.720
They deserve more respect,
more pay,

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:56.556
more of everything
in the classroom.

00:05:56.556 --> 00:05:58.291
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Absolutely.

00:05:58.291 --> 00:06:00.793
Your --
Your father was an educator.

00:06:00.793 --> 00:06:04.697
-Yes.
-Did you try to borrow anything

00:06:04.697 --> 00:06:07.299
in your character from the way
your father taught?

00:06:07.299 --> 00:06:09.868
-My dad is my lifelong angel.

00:06:09.868 --> 00:06:11.804
He was a lifelong learner.

00:06:11.804 --> 00:06:13.906
In fact,
when I hit that center stage,

00:06:13.906 --> 00:06:16.074
and I was about to ready
to just break down,

00:06:16.074 --> 00:06:18.344
I could hear my father say,
"Don't you dare.

00:06:18.344 --> 00:06:19.344
This is your moment.

00:06:19.344 --> 00:06:21.047
Make sure
they know who you are."

00:06:21.047 --> 00:06:22.148
[ Laughter ]

00:06:22.148 --> 00:06:24.416
I could hear him.
But for Barbara Howard,

00:06:24.416 --> 00:06:27.486
I really, really lean
into my Auntie Carolyn,

00:06:27.486 --> 00:06:31.290
who was a reluctant teacher
turned blue-ribbon principal

00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:33.693
who actually had
the Queen of England

00:06:33.693 --> 00:06:37.997
at her school twice because
she dared to ask her to come.

00:06:37.997 --> 00:06:40.065
-Oh, man, that's amazing.
And to come back.

00:06:40.065 --> 00:06:42.568
-How about that?
-Yeah.

00:06:42.568 --> 00:06:44.002
And sip that tea.

00:06:44.002 --> 00:06:45.704
I was like,
"Yes, Auntie Carolyn!

00:06:45.704 --> 00:06:48.507
You go, Bunker Hill.
She did that."

00:06:48.507 --> 00:06:50.409
-We talked about your
relationship with the stage.

00:06:50.409 --> 00:06:52.711
This is very exciting.
You're producing a play

00:06:52.711 --> 00:06:53.913
that's, I believe, starting --

00:06:53.913 --> 00:06:54.980
is it starting previews
in a couple weeks?

00:06:54.980 --> 00:06:57.049
-It's starting previews
in 11 days

00:06:57.049 --> 00:07:00.353
in the new
James Earl Jones Theatre.

00:07:00.353 --> 00:07:02.054
-Well, that's fantastic.
-Yes.

00:07:02.054 --> 00:07:03.255
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:03.255 --> 00:07:05.490
-I know this is a --
this is a play

00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:08.427
by a very prolific playwright
who we might not know.

00:07:08.427 --> 00:07:12.964
But what's really impressive is
how long she's been doing this.

00:07:12.964 --> 00:07:14.500
-You know what?
I tell people,

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:16.902
"If you don't make it at 20,
you can make it at 30.

00:07:16.902 --> 00:07:19.438
You can make it at 40.
You can make it at 50.

00:07:19.438 --> 00:07:21.573
You can make it at 60!"

00:07:21.573 --> 00:07:25.411
Well, the writer of this play,
"The Ohio State Murders,"

00:07:25.411 --> 00:07:29.782
Ms. Kennedy,
she is 91 years old.

00:07:29.782 --> 00:07:32.050
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:32.050 --> 00:07:36.055
So don't you ever,
ever give up on your dreams.

00:07:36.055 --> 00:07:37.756
She's 91 years old.

00:07:37.756 --> 00:07:38.891
I'm like, "You go, girl."

00:07:38.891 --> 00:07:40.559
Of course
I got to be a part of this.

00:07:40.559 --> 00:07:44.596
And Audra McDonald, too.
And directed by Kenny Leon.

00:07:44.596 --> 00:07:47.132
I'm like, "Come on.
We got to get halfway to EGOT.

00:07:47.132 --> 00:07:49.101
At least halfway."
-Yeah.

00:07:49.101 --> 00:07:51.203
Just don't try to do it
all in one year.

00:07:51.203 --> 00:07:54.173
-There you go.
But one never knows.

00:07:54.173 --> 00:07:56.175
-You could be the first EGOT
in one calendar year.

00:07:56.175 --> 00:07:57.709
-How about that?
That would be good.

00:07:57.709 --> 00:08:00.680
-That would be not bad.
-'22 has been a very good year.

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:05.751
-Well, maybe you'll grab the G
on your new Christmas song.

00:08:05.751 --> 00:08:07.353
What is the title
of your new Christmas song?

00:08:07.353 --> 00:08:08.554
-"Sleigh."
-"Sleigh"?

00:08:08.554 --> 00:08:09.688
-"Sleigh."
-Okay.

00:08:09.688 --> 00:08:11.790
But not S-L-A-Y.
-No.

00:08:11.790 --> 00:08:13.993
S-L-E-I-G-H.
-Okay.

00:08:13.993 --> 00:08:15.894
-But it all depends
on how you say it,

00:08:15.894 --> 00:08:17.930
because we are going to slay.

00:08:17.930 --> 00:08:19.198
[ Laughter ]

00:08:19.198 --> 00:08:21.233
-Yeah, I've never heard
Santa say it like that.

00:08:21.233 --> 00:08:23.302
[ Laughter ]

00:08:23.302 --> 00:08:24.770
He's never said it like that.

00:08:24.770 --> 00:08:26.471
Well, I'm very excited
to hear that.

00:08:26.471 --> 00:08:28.407
And, man, what an honor
just to see you again.

00:08:28.407 --> 00:08:29.909
-Oh, thank you.
-Please come back soon.

00:08:29.909 --> 00:08:32.445
Everyone,
that's Sheryl Lee Ralph.

00:08:32.445 --> 00:08:35.982
"Abbott Elementary,"
Wednesdays at 9:00 p.m., on ABC.

00:08:35.982 --> 00:08:39.552
We'll be right back
with Sohla El-Waylly.

